        <headline>Flood Watch issued September 30 at 4:46PM MDT until October 1 at 6:00AM MDT by NWS Grand Junction CO</headline>
        <description>* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be
possible.

* WHERE...Portions of Colorado, including the following areas, Animas
River Basin, Four Corners/Upper Dolores River, Northwest San Juan
Mountains, Paradox Valley/Lower Dolores River, San Juan River
Basin, Southwest San Juan Mountains and Uncompahgre Plateau and
Dallas Divide and southeast Utah, including the following areas,
Canyonlands/Natural Bridges, La Sal and Abajo Mountains and
Southeast Utah.

* WHEN...Through late tonight.

* IMPACTS...Flooding will be possible over burn scars, at low-water
crossings, and along creeks, streams, washes, arroyos and other
flood-prone locations. Creeks and streams may rise out of their
banks. Unpaved roads could become muddy and impassable.

* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...
- Periods of light to moderate rainfall, associated with remnant
moisture from Hurricane Polo, are expected to continue through
early this evening with isolated thunderstorms possible. Heavy
rain falling on saturated soils and burn scars could lead to
localized flash flooding and rises on local rivers. Rainfall is
expected to end by this evening, but rivers may rise out of their
banks overnight tonight even after rainfall has ended.
- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood
</description>
        <instruction>You should monitor later forecasts and be alert for possible Flood
Warnings. Those living in areas prone to flooding should be prepared
to take action should flooding develop.</instruction>
